class.description:
Put test scripts in the project's [code]tests[/code] folder (set by [code]testing/tests_folder[/code]), named so they end in [code]_test.gd[/code] or start with [code]test_[/code]. Every method whose name starts with [code]test_[/code] is a test: [codeblock] extends TestCase
var score: Score
func _before_each(): score = Score.new()
func test_adds_points(): score.add(5) score.add(3) assert_equal(score.points, 8)
func test_says_when_there_is_a_new_best(): (func(): score.add(1)).call_deferred() assert_true(await wait_for(score.new_best)) [/codeblock] Run them with [code]purr --headless --run-tests[/code]. Tests run in the order they're written, each with [method _before_each] and [method _after_each] round it. A failed assertion records what went wrong and the line it was on, and the test carries on, so one run shows every failure. A script error fails the test it happens in. Tests can [code]await[/code]: frames, signals, timers, or the wait methods here. A test that hasn't finished after [code]testing/timeout_seconds[/code] (30 by default) fails. The test script is a [Node] in the scene tree while it runs. Nodes a test adds as its children are freed after it, so each test starts clean. [code]--run-tests=text[/code] runs only test files whose paths contain [code]text[/code], or, if none do, only tests whose names contain it. [code]--test-report=path.xml[/code] also writes a JUnit report, which CI systems show. Purr exits with 1 if a test failed, a test file couldn't run, or no tests were found.
